  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nResidual low to mid clouds over eastern Kansas and surface ridge \naxis will migrate slowly east tonight. Temperatures will be warmer \non Friday with westerly downslope effects helping boost daytime \nhighs. Current satellite water vapor imagery shows strong jetstream \nenergy spreading from the eastern Pacific into the northwest \ncoastline with a series of upper waves embedded in this flow. Models \nare highlighting a healthy PV wave emerging from this jetstream\nenergy and moving onshore Friday night. This system will race\neast across the Rockies and eject out into the central plains \non Saturday. The fleet-footed nature of this system sprinting\neast will result in lighter precipitation amounts over Kansas. \nThermal profiles favors cold rain as the dominate precipitation \ntype, however there could be a brief period of snow trying to \nmix in. Little to no impacts are anticipated Saturday night if\nsome snow does briefly mix in. Additionally, north winds will \nramp up behind a southward racing cold front for Saturday night \ninto Sunday morning.\n\nDry weather with a gradual warming trend should be in store for \nKansas next week as upper flow becomes more zonal and south winds \nusher warmer air back into the region. Meanwhile, long range model \nensembles are showing another weather system possibly impacting\nthe central plains region late next week. The track and \nstrength of this upper wave coming from the southwest would have\na more favorable track for tapping into deeper Gulf of Mexico \nmoisture. Which could bring meaningful precipitation to the \nregion, however what is uncertain at this time is the exact \ntrack and locations that will be impacted from this wave. We \nwill continue to monitor model trends.",
